    "content": "The head of US Africa Command (AFRICOM), General Dagvin R.M. Anderson, has issued a warning that Somalia's political infighting could undermine the future of American military assistance to the country. In recent remarks, Anderson underscored that the sustained engagement of US forces in Somalia is contingent upon resolving internal political conflicts. He noted that the elite Danab commando unit, which has been a focal point of US training and funding, is now fully operational, but its long-term sustainability will depend on Somali leadership and responsibility.\n\nSomalia's strategic position along critical global shipping routes remains a key factor in Washington's interest in the region. Anderson highlighted that the country's geography is vital to US security priorities, making its stability an important concern. The general also addressed the US airstrike campaign, defending its accuracy and dismissing claims of civilian casualties as often exaggerated by Al-Shabaab, the militant group that continues to pose a significant threat.\n\nDespite years of sustained military pressure, Al-Shabaab has proven resilient, though it has been pushed back from areas near the capital, Mogadishu. The warning from Anderson comes at a time when Somalia faces political tensions that could distract from security efforts. The message is clear: without a cohesive political front, the gains achieved through US support may be at risk.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development carries particular weight. Many in the diaspora have family and business ties in Somalia, and ongoing instability can affect remittances, travel, and investment. The future of US military backing directly influences the security environment that shapes the lives of relatives back home and the potential for political progress. A reduction in support could embolden extremist groups and destabilize regions where diaspora members have roots, highlighting the interconnectedness of political unity and security.\n\nAs the US military assistance hangs in the balance, Somalia's leaders face a critical choice between internal discord and the preservation of a partnership that has been instrumental in counterterrorism operations. The outcome will not only affect the country's immediate security but also its long-term trajectory.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/africom-somalia-infighting-us-military-support/",
